\section{Introduction}
\iftoggle{plannthesis}{
	\emph{This is the `planning' version of this document. I got sick of maintaining multiple different \TeX\thinspace documents for various purposes, each duplicating various things from the others, so I will be using one single document with conditional compilation from now on. And giving it the cool title of `living draft' to make it sound special. This will be the planning report, half-time report, and final thesis.}
}{}

This thesis project will develop the idea of \textit{E-graphs Modulo Theories}, as proposed by \citet{zucker_omelets_2025}.

E-graphs are a data structure for \textit{equational theorem proving}. An E-graph represents a set of \textit{terms} and a set of equations known to hold among these terms. By a process of adding terms and equations to an E-graph, interesting equations can be discovered that are a consequence of a known theory.

E-graphs have seen a lot of interest since the publication of the \textsc{Egg} library developed by Willsey et al. and their corresponding paper \cite{willsey_egg_2021}. However, E-graphs struggle with a combinatorial explosion when reasoning about certain theories. An example of such a theory is any theory containin an associative and commutative operator: E-graphs can take significant time and space just to deduce basic consequences of the associative and commutative identities.

Zucker \citep{zucker_omelets_2025} proposes extending the notion of E-graphs to that of \textit{E-graphs Modulo Theories} (EMTs) in order to combine efficient methods for certain (sub)theories with the generic reasoning capabilities of E-graphs. Zucker lists a number of subtheories which can potentially be handled more efficiently, including associativity and commutativity, polynomials, and vector spaces.

Unfortunately, the algorithm presented in \citet{zucker_omelets_2025} is both inefficient and incomplete, in ways that we will make clear. We will develop the formal notion of EM$(T)$ for a theory $T$, and give a set of requirements on $T$ for when EM$(T)$ can be provide a complete and potentially more efficient alternative to E-graphs over a theory including $T$. To achieve this, Zucker's requirement of a \emph{canonizer} needs to be strengthened to having a \emph{solver} for the word problem.

We will show how to build a solver for the theory AC of 1 or more associative and commutative operators, as well as the closely related theories of \fxerror{check that this list is good once we finalize} AC with unit elements (ACU) and polynomials.

We then discuss an implementation of EMTs and benchmark it against pure E-graph reasoning on a variety of tasks involving these theories.

\subsection{Outline}
