Section \ref{background} describes equational theorem proving (section \ref{eqthm}) and the how E-graphs can prove equations (section \ref{egraphs}). Section \ref{eqsat} describes the \textit{equality saturation} outer loop for E-graphs. \ref{acegg} discusses the problems with using E-graphs directly with associative and/or commutative operators.

\ref{modtheor} discusses reasoning modulo theories. \ref{coreres} describes in detail what is necessary for EMTs, and how EMTs can be implemented for our example theories of associativity and commutativity.

\ref{relq} discusses the relational queries and how matching over E-graphs, an important step in equality saturation, is \textit{conjunctive query} or \textit{join}. \ref{joind} builds a diagrammatic formalism for describing methods to evaluate and optimize joins. We discuss how to extend efficient techniques for evaluating joins to the case of \textit{matching modulo theories} \ref{mmt}.

\ref{impl} discusses our implementation and uses it to benchmark various strategies for handling associativity, commutativity, and both together.

