\section{E-graphs} \label{background}
\subsection{Logic and (equational) theorem proving}
Theorem-proving in logic is a central topic in computer science \fxerror{source}. Equational theorem proving is a restriction to a particular fragment: we are given a set of terms, a set of \emph{equations}, and a set of identities, and we try to determine if some equation follows from the conjunction of these equations and identities.

An equation between terms $s$ and $t$ will be written as $s = t$. An identity is a universally quantified equation: for instance, $\forall x y. x + y = y + x$ is an identity that holds universally in $x$ and $y$. An identity represents the set of all of its \emph{ground instances}, which are equations derived from substituting terms for the variables. For instance, if $s$ and $t$ are terms, $s + t = t + s$ is an instance of the previous identity.

Automatic equational theorem proving is about finding algorithms to determine if some equation follows from the conjunction of a set of identities and equations. In this thesis we will not consider the more general task of automatically determining if an identity follows from them.

\subsection{Equivalence relations and Union-find}
Given a set, an \textbf{equivalence relation} over that set if a reflexive, symmetric, and transitive equation. Given an equivalence relation, we can take the \textbf{quotient} of the set by it, a set containing a single element for each \textbf{equivalence class} of elements in the original set.

The \emph{Union-Find} data structure is a very efficient means to represent an equivalence relation over a set, and to update it either by extending the underlying set of by merging two equivalence classes.

Figure \ref{fig:eqrel1} shows a union-find structure that assigns the name $e_1$ to the equivalence class that encompasses the terms $a$, $b$, and $c$, and the name $e_2$ to the equivalence class containing just the term $d$.

