\section{Join diagrams}
\subsection{Diagrams of relations}
We represent relations diagramatically as follows. For a relation $R$ with arity 2, we use a box with wires like the following:

\begin{tikzpicture}[
	marker/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=90, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
]
	\node[box, box ports east=2] (R) {R};
	\foreach \side in {east}
		\foreach \index in {1,...,2}
			\node[marker] at (R.\side.\index) {};
	\wires{}{R.east.1, R.east.2}
\end{tikzpicture}

We also allow a relation to have `left' and `right' wires, representing the `input' and `output' sides. A box with $n$ left wires and $m$ right wires is a set of $n + m$-tuples. The input/output distinction does not matter yet: the semantics is still the set of all tuples.

Here is a (2, 2) relation:

\begin{tikzpicture}[
	markerr/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=90, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
	markerl/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=270, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
]
	\node[box, box ports east=2, box ports west=2] (R) {R};
	\foreach \side in {east}
		\foreach \index in {1,...,2}
			\node[markerr] at (R.\side.\index) {};sty
	\foreach \side in {west}
		\foreach \index in {1,...,2}
			\node[markerl] at (R.\side.\index) {};
	\wires{}{R.east.1, R.east.2, R.west.1, R.west.2}
\end{tikzpicture}


The product of two relations $R$ and $S$ is $\{(r, s)\;|\;r \in R, s \in S\}$ and is represented as the vertical juxtaposition of the diagrams:

\begin{tikzpicture}[
	markerr/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=90, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
	markerl/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=270, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
]
	\node[box, box ports west=1, box ports east=2] (R) at (0, 1) {R};
	\node[box, box ports east=3] (S) at (0, 0) {S};
	\foreach \side in {east} {
		\foreach \index in {1,...,2}
			\node[markerr] at (R.\side.\index) {};
		\foreach \index in {1,...,3}
			\node[markerr] at (S.\side.\index) {};
	}
	\foreach \side in {west}
		\foreach \index in {1}
			\node[markerl] at (R.\side.\index) {};
	\wires{}{R.east.1, R.east.2, S.east.1, S.east.2, S.east.3, R.west.1}
\end{tikzpicture}

The composition of an $(n, m)$- and an $(m, r)$-relation is a $(n, r)$-relation, and is where the input/output distinction becomes useful. The composition of $R$ and $S$ is $\{(x, z)\mid\exists y. (x, y) \in R, (y, z) \in S\}$.

\begin{tikzpicture}[
	markerr/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=90, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
	markerl/.style={semicircle, anchor=chord center, shape border rotate=270, draw=black, fill=white, inner sep=1pt},
]
	\node[box, box ports west=1, box ports east=2] (R) at (0, 0) {R};
	\node[box, box ports west=2, box ports east=1] (S) at (1, 0) {S};
	\node[markerr] at (R.east.1) {};
	\node[markerr] at (R.east.2) {};
	\node[markerl] at (S.west.1) {};
	\node[markerl] at (S.west.2) {};
	\wires{R = {east.1 = S.west.1, east.2 = S.west.2}}{R.west.1, S.east.1}
\end{tikzpicture}

The equality relation, defined by $\{((x, y), z)\mid x = y = z\}$ is represented as just a circle:

\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[dot] (d0) at (1, 1) {};
  \draw (1, 1) to[out=0, in=180] (2, 1) (d0.center);
  \draw (d0.center) to[out=90, in=0] (0, 2);
  \draw (d0.center) to[out=-90, in=0] (0, 0);
\end{tikzpicture}

We have versions of equality with any number of input and output wires. For instance, with the input/output idea in mind, the following diagram duplicates the value on the input wire:


\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[dot] (d0) at (1, 1) {};
  \draw (1, 1) to[out=0, in=180] (0, 1) (d0.center);
  \draw (d0.center) to[out=90, in=180] (2, 2);
  \draw (d0.center) to[out=-90, in=180] (2, 0);
\end{tikzpicture}

With equality, composition, and product, we can represent the relational join:

